Written offer for complete corresponding source
===============================================

This archive contains opengrads-hpc @DIST_VERSION@, a binary distribution of
GrADS @GRADS_VERSION@ and its supporting libraries. GrADS is licensed under
the GNU General Public License, version 2; see the accompanying COPYING file.

GPLv2 section 3 requires that a binary distribution be accompanied by the
complete corresponding source code, or by a written offer to supply it. This
file is that written offer.

Complete corresponding source
-----------------------------

The complete corresponding source for the GrADS executable and the graphics
plug-ins in this archive, including the scripts used to control compilation
and installation, is the following commit of the public repository:

    Repository: @REPO_URL@
    Commit:     @COMMIT@
    Built:      @BUILD_ID@

Every third-party dependency bundled here is built from an unmodified,
checksum-pinned upstream release. The exact version, download URL, and
SHA-256 of each one is recorded in `release/versions.env` at the commit
above, and the build that consumes them is `release/build-dependencies.sh`.
Platform libraries taken from Homebrew (macOS) or MSYS2 (Windows) are
unmodified upstream builds; `licenses/` records what this archive carries.

The offer
---------

For a period of three years from the date this archive was distributed, the
maintainer will supply, to any third party who asks, a complete
machine-readable copy of the corresponding source described above, for no
more than the cost of physically performing the distribution.

Requests may be sent to:

    @CONTACT@

If the repository above is reachable, downloading that commit is the fastest
way to obtain the same source and requires no request.

Licensing note
--------------

See THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md in this archive for the licenses of every bundled
component, and for a documented, unresolved GPLv2/Apache-2.0 compatibility
question affecting the ADIOS2 BP5 support in this binary. Building from
source does not carry that question.
